      "lastReply": "A young tiger named Raja lived deep in the jungle, where he spent his days hunting and roaming. One evening, he caught the scent of something magical drifting through the trees—spices, rice, and slow-cooked meat. He followed it to a small dhaba at the forest's edge, where an old cook was making biryani. Raja watched, mesmerized, as the man layered rice, marinated chicken, and saffron in a heavy pot. The cook noticed the tiger's curious eyes and, instead of running, smiled. \"Want to learn?\" he asked.\n\nRaja returned every night. The cook taught him to toast whole spices until they crackled, to marinate meat with yogurt and ginger, and to seal the pot with dough so the steam stayed trapped inside. Raja's paws were clumsy at first, but he practiced. He learned to judge the heat by smell, to wait patiently while the biryani cooked, and to fluff the rice gently so every grain stayed separate. The other animals thought he was mad, but Raja didn't care. He had found something better than any hunt.\n\nOne day, the cook fell ill. Raja took over the dhaba, cooking biryani for travelers who stopped by. They were shocked to see a tiger in an apron, but one bite silenced them. Word spread, and soon people came from distant towns just to taste the biryani made by the tiger who had learned to cook. Raja never went back to hunting. He had discovered that the best meals are the ones you make with your own hands—or paws.\n\n9:24 PM",
